{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUU3tHPn7qeCVmkbYNVG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Bottom line: use the 3PL now.** The numbers don't support building yet, and a split only makes sense after you already have a warehouse to fill.\n\n**The math**\n\n- West coast volume: 35% × 4,500/week ≈ 1,575 orders/week ≈ 82,000/year\n- 3PL cost at current volume: 82,000 × $3.10 ≈ **$254,000/year**, scales linearly, no capital risk\n- Warehouse fixed cost: $420,000 lease + 8 hires (assuming ~$50K fully loaded each ≈ $400K) ≈ **$820,000/year**, before racking, WMS software, utilities, insurance, or packaging supplies — so treat that as a floor, not a ceiling\n\n**Breakeven volume**: $820,000 ÷ $3.10 ≈ 264,500 orders/year ≈ 5,100 orders/week on the west coast — roughly **3.2x your current west-coast volume** (or ~14,600 total weekly orders company-wide if the 35% mix holds). Below that, the 3PL is cheaper on cost alone; the warehouse only starts paying for itself past that line, and it's a 3-year lease so you're locked in either way.\n\n**The tradeoff that isn't in the math**: the 3PL gives up control over packaging (brand/unboxing experience) and returns handling. If either is a real differentiator or cost driver for you today, that's worth paying a premium for — but the premium here is roughly $570K/year at current volume, which is a lot to pay for control before you've proven the volume is there.\n\n**Recommendation**: go 3PL now, and set a trigger to revisit — once west-coast volume approaches ~5,000+ orders/week (or you have visibility into growth that gets you there within the 3-year window), build the warehouse. A \"split\" doesn't make sense as a starting position; it's a good pattern *after* you have a warehouse, sending overflow/peak volume to a 3PL rather than hiring for peak capacity.\n\nOne gap: I assumed ~$50K fully loaded per hire since it wasn't given — if actual wages are meaningfully different, or you have a specific west-coast growth rate, I can redo the breakeven with real numbers."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":40626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc061a12-c583-4e68-95de-dcb1a3e4b80a","uuid":"27b30809-9765-457a-977a-acc6a15a6199","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:06:48.057Z","request_id":"req_011CfUU3sx3d9AaETA5mCfbH"}
